LIKEOS-EXPLORER(1)               User Commands              LIKEOS-EXPLORER(1)

NAME
       likeos-explorer - browse and manage files and folders

SYNOPSIS
       likeos-explorer [location...]
       likeos-explorer  --copy-to=folder | --move-to=folder | --link-to=folder
       file...
       likeos-explorer --trash | --delete file...
       likeos-explorer --empty-trash
       likeos-explorer --version

DESCRIPTION
       likeos-explorer is LikeOS Explorer, the  desktop's  file  manager:  the
       window  a folder opens in from the desktop, the Programs menu, the win-
       dow manager's menu and any program that opens a folder (xdg-open).  The
       desktop itself stays PCManFM's.

       Each invocation opens a window, with a tab for every location given, or
       Home when none is.  A location is a folder (a path, a URI or ~/...),  a
       file  (its  folder  opens  with  the  file selected), trash:/// for the
       Trash, computer:/// (or likeos:computer) for Computer,  or  likeos:home
       for  Home.  A second invocation hands its locations to the running pro-
       gram over the session bus, so all windows share one list of file opera-
       tions, one undo history and one clipboard.

OPTIONS
       With one of these options no window opens: the operation  runs  in  the
       File  operations  window  like any other, and the program keeps running
       until it is done.  The desktop hands its copies, moves, links, moves to
       the Trash and deletions over this way.

       --copy-to=folder file...
              Copy the files into folder.

       --move-to=folder file...
              Move the files into folder.

       --link-to=folder file...
              Make symbolic links to the files in folder.

       --trash file...
              Move the files to the Trash.

       --delete file...
              Delete the files permanently.  Nothing is asked first.

       --empty-trash
              Delete everything in the Trash.  Nothing is asked first.

       --version
              Show the version.

THE WINDOW
       Tabs   Each tab is a place of its own with its own history.

       Address row
              Back, Forward (right-click for the history), Up and Refresh; the
              address bar, whose crumbs open their  folder  and  whose  arrows
              list  the folders inside, and which turns into a text field on a
              click beside the crumbs; and the search box, which searches  the
              folder shown and everything below it.

       Toolbar
              New,  Cut,  Copy,  Paste, Rename, Share, Delete, Sort, View, and
              more behind the three dots; at the right end  the  switches  for
              the  details  and the preview pane.  What the selection does not
              allow is greyed out.  In the Trash it offers  Empty  Trash,  Re-
              store and Restore all.

       Navigation pane
              Three  groups:  Favorites  (Home, Gallery, the personal folders,
              the pinned folders -- the GTK bookmarks, shared with every  file
              dialog  -- and the Trash), Devices (Computer and its drives) and
              Network when a backend provides it.  Files dropped on a row  are
              copied or moved there.

       Content
              Home  shows the pinned and the frequently used folders as cards,
              and the recently used files and the Favorites.   Computer  shows
              the drives with their free space.  A folder is shown as Details,
              List,  Compact,  Icons,  Large icons (with thumbnails) or Tiles;
              the choice is remembered per folder.

       Details and preview panes
              The selected item's picture, type, size, dates and place, or its
              contents: an image, the beginning of a text, a video's frame and
              length.

   Searching
       Words in the search box must all appear in a name; case does  not  mat-
       ter.  * and ?  match any characters.  kind:image (or video, audio, doc-
       ument,  folder, archive, program) and ext:png filter by kind and exten-
       sion.  Below the home folder an index answers at  once;  elsewhere  the
       folders are searched as the results come in.

KEYS
       Ctrl+T, Ctrl+W, Ctrl+N        new tab, close tab, new window
       Alt+Left, Alt+Right, Alt+Up   back, forward, up
       Ctrl+L, Alt+D, F4             edit the address
       Ctrl+F, Ctrl+E, F3            search
       F5                            refresh
       Ctrl+X, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+V        cut, copy, paste
       Ctrl+Shift+C                  copy the path
       F2                            rename
       Delete                        move to the Trash
       Shift+Delete                  delete permanently
       Ctrl+Shift+N                  new folder
       Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y                undo, redo
       Ctrl+A                        select all
       Alt+Enter                     properties
       Alt+P, Alt+Shift+P            preview pane, details pane
       Ctrl+H                        show hidden items
       Ctrl+Shift+2...8              the views of the View menu
       Ctrl+wheel                    smaller or larger view

       Typing a name in a folder selects the first item that starts with it.

FILE OPERATIONS
       Copies,  moves,  deletions  and the rest run in the background, at most
       two at a time and never two into the same folder at once.  An operation
       that takes more than a moment opens the File operations  window,  which
       shows each one's progress, a graph of its speed, the time left and what
       remains,  and  can  pause  or cancel it.  Fewer details leaves only the
       progress bars for as long as the window stays open; it opens  with  the
       graph  again  the next time.  The program keeps running while an opera-
       tion is not finished or the window shows, also with no Explorer  window
       open.

       When a name is taken at the destination, Explorer shows the item coming
       in  beside  the one already there and asks whether to replace it, skip,
       keep both (the new item gets a number), or for two folders merge  them,
       with  a  choice  to answer the same way for the rest.  A failure can be
       retried, skipped or the whole operation cancelled.

       A file is copied under a hidden temporary name and renamed  into  place
       when complete, so an interrupted or cancelled copy leaves no half-writ-
       ten  file.  A move between drives removes the original only when all of
       it arrived.

       Undo reverses the last copy, move, rename, new item, move to the  Trash
       or  restore:  copies  and  new items go to the Trash, moved and renamed
       items go back, trashed items are restored.   Deleting  permanently  and
       emptying the Trash cannot be undone.

FILES
       ~/.local/share/Trash
              The Trash (freedesktop.org layout).

       ~/.config/gtk-3.0/bookmarks
              Pinned folders.

       ~/.local/share/recently-used.xbel
              Recently used files, shared by every program.  Explorer settings
              can turn their display off or clear the list.

       ~/.cache/thumbnails
              Thumbnails, shared with the desktop.

       ~/.cache/likeos-explorer/index-v1
              The  search  index  of  the home folder.  Turned off in Explorer
              settings, it is deleted.

       ~/.config/glib-2.0/settings/keyfile
              Settings (schema org.likeos.Explorer).

SEE ALSO
